00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:36.490
The solution we're building is
an infrastructure as a service

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:40.510
solution, and it's
based on our IBM Power

00:05:40.510 --> 00:05:45.670
Systems running our PowerVM
virtualization stack.

00:05:45.670 --> 00:05:47.530
The subscription
model is actually

00:05:47.530 --> 00:05:52.580
a capacity for subscription
model with a monthly price,

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:55.990
and it is built on a concept
we called a cloud instance.

00:05:55.990 --> 00:05:59.790
So think of a cloud instance
as a bundle of compute,

00:05:59.790 --> 00:06:02.670
memory, storage, and network.

00:06:02.670 --> 00:06:04.560
You then take that
cloud instance

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:06.750
and we give you
a user experience

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:09.300
so that you can
create and manage

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:12.690
one or more virtual
machines within that slice

00:06:12.690 --> 00:06:14.170
of infrastructure.

00:06:14.170 --> 00:06:15.730
So that's a cloud instance.

00:06:15.730 --> 00:06:19.440
You might also hear me refer
to it as a t-shirt as we go

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:22.590
through the presentation,
because we think of them like

00:06:22.590 --> 00:06:23.700
t-shirt sizes--

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:27.140
small, medium, large,
extra large, and so forth.

00:06:27.140 --> 00:06:28.900
OK.

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:32.810
Now in the solution, IBM
runs the infrastructure.

00:06:32.810 --> 00:06:37.810
So we're managing the hardware,
the systems software stack--

00:06:37.810 --> 00:06:40.440
if you're familiar
with power systems,

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:44.290
those different hardware
elements like our HMC,

00:06:44.290 --> 00:06:45.080
and so forth.

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:47.220
So we're taking care of that--

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:50.400
maintaining it, patching it--

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:52.980
but once the virtual machine
is deployed with a running

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:56.790
operating system, it's the
subscriber's responsibility

00:06:56.790 --> 00:06:58.620
to manage and
maintain the operating

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:00.510
system in the stack up.

00:07:00.510 --> 00:07:02.980
I want to just do one quick
show of hands-- how many of you

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:06.300
are familiar with power
systems terminology

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:08.820
like LPARs and HMCs?

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:09.450
OK, great.

00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:13.570
So I'm talking to a crowd
who knows our vocabulary.

00:07:13.570 --> 00:07:14.070
Very good.

00:07:16.820 --> 00:07:19.220
The last part of this
solution involves

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:23.690
a low-latency interconnect,
or network, between our power

00:07:23.690 --> 00:07:27.350
infrastructure footprint
and GCP And this gives you

00:07:27.350 --> 00:07:32.240
a private low-latency
network between GCP

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:34.920
resources and power resources.

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:38.660
So for example, if you need
to run a database on power

00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:40.600
on the back end, and
you want to run your app

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:43.790
tier on GCP compute--

00:07:43.790 --> 00:07:45.380
they're close to
each other, they're

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:47.240
connected on a
low-latency network,

00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:49.910
and you can run that
hybrid workflow.

00:07:49.910 --> 00:07:54.160
If you need to store your
data in GCP cloud storage,

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:55.350
you've got a private--

00:07:55.350 --> 00:07:57.170
you're inside the
Google network.

00:07:57.170 --> 00:08:02.120
You can use GCP cloud storage
from your AIX instance,

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:02.660
for example.

00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:11.150
While it is an IBM
solution, you'll

00:08:11.150 --> 00:08:14.460
subscribe to it through
GCP marketplace.

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:17.485
So just like you subscribe to
other resources and offerings

00:08:17.485 --> 00:08:18.860
through the
marketplace, there'll

00:08:18.860 --> 00:08:21.200
be a tile for power systems.

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:24.610
You see an example
draft of that tile here.

00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:28.040
And this is where you'll go
and see the different cloud

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:31.540
plans that you can purchase--
the cloud instance plans.

00:08:31.540 --> 00:08:33.590
And here's just a
simple example of what

00:08:33.590 --> 00:08:38.900
we might call a starter cloud
with 16 cores, 160 gig of RAM,

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:40.909
and 12 terabytes of
storage, and then you

00:08:40.909 --> 00:08:45.770
can take that and partition
that up into virtual machines.

00:08:45.770 --> 00:08:48.500
I mentioned earlier we think
of these in t-shirt sizes.

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:52.010
So an experience you'll be able
to have through the marketplace

00:08:52.010 --> 00:08:58.160
is that, as your needs grow, or
as your journey here evolves,

00:08:58.160 --> 00:08:59.180
you can change plans.

00:08:59.180 --> 00:09:01.718
So let's say you start
with a small t-shirt,

00:09:01.718 --> 00:09:03.260
and then you get to
a point where you

00:09:03.260 --> 00:09:05.070
realize you need more capacity.

00:09:05.070 --> 00:09:08.650
You can upgrade to a medium
t-shirt or a large t-shirt.

00:09:08.650 --> 00:09:11.420
And maybe you only need to do
that for a temporary basis,

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:14.600
and then you can go back, let's
say, from a medium t-shirt

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:15.740
to a small t-shirt.

00:09:15.740 --> 00:09:17.790
You can downgrade as well.

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:20.360
So the GCP marketplace
allows you to do that.

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:27.130
So what's under the hood?

00:09:27.130 --> 00:09:29.950
So we're starting with
Power9 technology-- that's

00:09:29.950 --> 00:09:33.130
our latest generation
of power, and we've

00:09:33.130 --> 00:09:35.690
built an infrastructure
concept we call a pod.

00:09:35.690 --> 00:09:39.190
Think of a pod as multiple
racks of equipment

00:09:39.190 --> 00:09:41.920
that we've put together for you.

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:45.370
While we're starting with
our two-socket 922 systems,

00:09:45.370 --> 00:09:47.890
our architecture
is such that we can

00:09:47.890 --> 00:09:51.010
incorporate any of our
PowerVM M-based servers

00:09:51.010 --> 00:09:52.700
into this environment.

00:09:52.700 --> 00:09:56.110
So we could put a
192 core 64 terabyte

00:09:56.110 --> 00:09:58.390
server in this
infrastructure, and it

00:09:58.390 --> 00:10:02.560
integrates into GCP the same
way that that two-socket server

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:04.290
does.

00:10:04.290 --> 00:10:07.930
It's fiber channel
connected storage.

00:10:07.930 --> 00:10:11.310
We're leveraging 25 gig
networking down at the servers,

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:13.960
100 gigabit across the
racks, and then that

00:10:13.960 --> 00:10:19.770
feeds up into an
interconnect tier to GCP.

00:10:19.770 --> 00:10:21.760
From a systems
software perspective,

00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:24.840
it's the same PowerVM
stack that you're probably

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:28.620
deploying in your own data
center, or if you're a partner,

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:32.880
it's what you've been building
and servicing for your clients.

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:37.440
My key point here is this is
the same type of infrastructure

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:41.110
that you deploy and use
in your own data centers.

00:10:41.110 --> 00:10:44.725
It's fully compatible,
that's by design,

00:10:44.725 --> 00:10:46.350
it's the same
infrastructure that we've

00:10:46.350 --> 00:10:49.680
done all our ISV certifications
with, and so forth.

00:10:55.580 --> 00:10:58.150
So to manage your
cloud instance,

00:10:58.150 --> 00:11:01.270
we've built a new management
experience completely

00:11:01.270 --> 00:11:06.760
from the ground up and it starts
with an open API compliant REST

00:11:06.760 --> 00:11:11.110
API that we've built. We've
actually built it on GCP.

00:11:11.110 --> 00:11:14.760
It actually runs on
Google Kubernetes engine.

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:17.730
It's a micro-services-based
architecture,

00:11:17.730 --> 00:11:21.460
and that API is what sits on top
of our infrastructure footprint

00:11:21.460 --> 00:11:25.720
and provides all the operations
for virtual machine lifecycle

00:11:25.720 --> 00:11:28.370
management and general
service management.

00:11:28.370 --> 00:11:31.660
So everything from
creating a new VM or LPAR,

00:11:31.660 --> 00:11:36.640
to stopping, starting, modifying
it, ultimately deleting it

00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:39.850
if it comes to end of
life, and we've also

00:11:39.850 --> 00:11:42.910
put in functionality around
managing your boot images,

00:11:42.910 --> 00:11:44.530
or your VM images--

00:11:44.530 --> 00:11:47.515
that you can bring your VM
images from your own data

00:11:47.515 --> 00:11:51.850
center up into the cloud, we'll
have some stock images for you,

00:11:51.850 --> 00:11:53.708
and then also managing
your storage--

00:11:53.708 --> 00:11:55.000
what we call your data volumes.

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:57.370
And Kyle is going to show
you a little bit more

00:11:57.370 --> 00:12:01.230
detail on that later
in the presentation.

00:12:01.230 --> 00:12:05.520
On top of that API, we've built
a couple of user interface

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:07.540
experiences for you.

00:12:07.540 --> 00:12:10.190
One is a web console experience.

00:12:10.190 --> 00:12:14.310
And you might notice it has a
similar material design to GCP

00:12:14.310 --> 00:12:16.350
compute, and that's on purpose.

00:12:16.350 --> 00:12:19.620
We want it to feel like a
natural extension of GCP

00:12:19.620 --> 00:12:20.700
for you.

00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:23.890
So it will look and feel
a lot like GCP compute,

00:12:23.890 --> 00:12:26.550
but it will also expose
some of the unique features

00:12:26.550 --> 00:12:28.530
of our power technology.

00:12:28.530 --> 00:12:32.850
For example, shared cores
versus dedicated cores.

00:12:32.850 --> 00:12:34.590
So where it makes
sense, we've actually

00:12:34.590 --> 00:12:36.510
made those
capabilities available

00:12:36.510 --> 00:12:39.075
for you to control as
you build and manage

00:12:39.075 --> 00:12:42.510
your virtual machines.

00:12:42.510 --> 00:12:46.120
We've also provided a
command line interface tool

00:12:46.120 --> 00:12:47.710
as part of the user experience.

00:12:47.710 --> 00:12:50.260
And one interesting
feedback that we've had-- we

00:12:50.260 --> 00:12:54.940
did a POC last year with
several of our clients,

00:12:54.940 --> 00:12:58.750
and we had overwhelming
feedback that an API in a CLI

00:12:58.750 --> 00:13:01.930
was more important than
a point and click GUI.

00:13:01.930 --> 00:13:06.070
So we've actually put more
focus on our development efforts

00:13:06.070 --> 00:13:10.270
for the initial capability
around the command line

00:13:10.270 --> 00:13:11.710
interface, because
that's actually

00:13:11.710 --> 00:13:14.470
what the customers
told us they wanted.

00:13:14.470 --> 00:13:18.250
And the command line interface
we built is called pcloud.

00:13:18.250 --> 00:13:20.560
If you're used to
using gcloud compute,

00:13:20.560 --> 00:13:22.620
this is going to feel
very similar to you.

00:13:22.620 --> 00:13:25.030
It's just, again, a
natural extension,

00:13:25.030 --> 00:13:28.750
and it gives you a command line
model for that Virtual Machine

00:13:28.750 --> 00:13:29.730
Management.

00:13:35.660 --> 00:13:38.200
Once you've created
these virtual machines,

00:13:38.200 --> 00:13:40.440
you'll need to access them.

00:13:40.440 --> 00:13:43.930
And to do that, we've built
a very innovative data plane

00:13:43.930 --> 00:13:48.520
architecture in partnership
with Google that gives you

00:13:48.520 --> 00:13:50.590
a mechanism so
that you can access

00:13:50.590 --> 00:13:53.410
the virtual machines
in your cloud instance

00:13:53.410 --> 00:13:55.120
from a GCP project.

00:13:55.120 --> 00:13:57.190
And it's, again, a
natural extension

00:13:57.190 --> 00:13:59.840
of your virtual private
cloud set of networks.

00:13:59.840 --> 00:14:04.090
It's just another network that
gets integrated into your VPC

00:14:04.090 --> 00:14:06.580
and provides a
private IP address

00:14:06.580 --> 00:14:11.740
model to control the access
to your virtual machines.

00:14:11.740 --> 00:14:13.240
And I'm actually
going to bring Andy

00:14:13.240 --> 00:14:15.100
back up to take you
through a little bit

00:14:15.100 --> 00:14:17.560
more detail of the
set of technologies

00:14:17.560 --> 00:14:21.140
that have made this type
of integration possible.

00:14:21.140 --> 00:14:22.528
ANDY WADDELL: Thanks, Carl.

00:14:22.528 --> 00:14:24.800
I don't need that, thanks.

00:14:24.800 --> 00:14:27.253
So I mentioned this
earlier, and I'll just

00:14:27.253 --> 00:14:28.670
give you a little
bit more detail,

00:14:28.670 --> 00:14:29.780
and we can follow later.

00:14:29.780 --> 00:14:33.260
But at Google, we
have a lot of services

00:14:33.260 --> 00:14:37.400
that we develop natively that
follow a similar model where

00:14:37.400 --> 00:14:39.770
the service itself is
exposed over a range of IP

00:14:39.770 --> 00:14:44.992
addresses that you, as the
customer, need to specify,

00:14:44.992 --> 00:14:46.950
because you have your
own IP addressing scheme,

00:14:46.950 --> 00:14:50.420
you may have on-prem IP
ranges, but we're always

00:14:50.420 --> 00:14:54.840
talking about RFC 1918
private address space here.

00:14:54.840 --> 00:14:58.400
So, for the first time ever,
we made this technology

00:14:58.400 --> 00:15:01.820
available to a partner--

00:15:01.820 --> 00:15:03.280
IBM in this case--

00:15:03.280 --> 00:15:06.770
and the way it works, it's
based on an aggregation

00:15:06.770 --> 00:15:09.530
of several Google networking
technologies that were put

00:15:09.530 --> 00:15:11.550
together in an architecture.

00:15:11.550 --> 00:15:14.140
So if you were to subscribe
to this offer-- let's

00:15:14.140 --> 00:15:18.772
say you were client
1, you would first

00:15:18.772 --> 00:15:20.480
initiate the purchase
in the marketplace,

00:15:20.480 --> 00:15:22.940
and then you would
specify a private IP range

00:15:22.940 --> 00:15:24.470
that you wish to use.

00:15:24.470 --> 00:15:27.350
You would reserve that
inside your Google VPC,

00:15:27.350 --> 00:15:29.323
in your project, and
then we would give you

00:15:29.323 --> 00:15:30.740
a peering command,
which you would

00:15:30.740 --> 00:15:34.273
need to run because we
can't control your project.

00:15:34.273 --> 00:15:35.690
And what that
peering command does

00:15:35.690 --> 00:15:39.180
is it peers you to another
VPC inside of Google,

00:15:39.180 --> 00:15:43.250
but that's owned by IBM,
and that's the end of it.

00:15:43.250 --> 00:15:45.200
The rest of the
plumbing, network-wise,

00:15:45.200 --> 00:15:47.157
is handled by IBM.

00:15:47.157 --> 00:15:49.740
Obviously you saw they have real
hardware sitting behind this.

00:15:49.740 --> 00:15:52.880
So they handle the hybrid
network connectivity,

00:15:52.880 --> 00:15:55.370
attaching VLANs and all that,
but that's all transparent.

00:15:55.370 --> 00:16:02.630
All you see is some advertised
routes for the IBM LPARs,

00:16:02.630 --> 00:16:05.540
and similarly, your routes
on your private VLAN

00:16:05.540 --> 00:16:07.010
are advertised back.

00:16:07.010 --> 00:16:12.330
So we think that you'll find
it very, very simple to use.

00:16:12.330 --> 00:16:15.560
Most of the complexity is
handled by the provider--

00:16:15.560 --> 00:16:19.550
IBM in this case, and it's going
to be very similar to if you've

00:16:19.550 --> 00:16:22.990
ever used Google Cloud SQL.

00:16:22.990 --> 00:16:26.407
It's a very similar
workflow, and there's

00:16:26.407 --> 00:16:28.740
many more Google services
that are following this model.

00:16:33.210 --> 00:16:35.830
Oh, one thing to
point out a case

00:16:35.830 --> 00:16:38.300
is not completely obvious--

00:16:38.300 --> 00:16:43.090
there is no connectivity
into the IBM LPAR

00:16:43.090 --> 00:16:47.607
world from the internet without
going through Google Cloud

00:16:47.607 --> 00:16:48.190
to begin with.

00:16:48.190 --> 00:16:51.010
So that's important
for security,

00:16:51.010 --> 00:16:53.020
and it gives you
all the features

00:16:53.020 --> 00:16:56.020
that the Google front
end has in order

00:16:56.020 --> 00:16:59.855
to secure the workloads
that you're running in IBM.

00:16:59.855 --> 00:17:02.230
