00:26:47.650 --> 00:26:50.980
So that's been installed into
your cloud instance storage.

00:26:50.980 --> 00:26:53.260
We do have a way
here that IBM is

00:26:53.260 --> 00:26:56.050
going to keep a collection,
like a catalog of images,

00:26:56.050 --> 00:26:58.460
for you to use that
are stock images.

00:26:58.460 --> 00:26:59.958
So if you do the
available command,

00:26:59.958 --> 00:27:02.500
you'll be able to list all the
stock images available for you

00:27:02.500 --> 00:27:03.670
to copy into your cloud.

00:27:06.913 --> 00:27:08.080
Next we'll look at networks.

00:27:12.010 --> 00:27:14.640
So you can see here there's
that default gcpnetwork

00:27:14.640 --> 00:27:15.958
that I spoke of earlier--

00:27:15.958 --> 00:27:18.000
let's go ahead and do a
describe on that network.

00:27:22.395 --> 00:27:24.270
So you can see here I
can get the information

00:27:24.270 --> 00:27:26.970
about my CIDR, my
IP ranges that will

00:27:26.970 --> 00:27:29.355
be assigned to my partitions
when they're created,

00:27:29.355 --> 00:27:30.370
I have my gateway--

00:27:30.370 --> 00:27:32.340
you can also assign
any type of DNS

00:27:32.340 --> 00:27:34.035
that you would like
for your instances.

00:27:34.035 --> 00:27:35.910
So you might actually
have a cloud DNS that's

00:27:35.910 --> 00:27:38.647
running inside GCP that
you can do the routing for,

00:27:38.647 --> 00:27:40.980
or you can even maybe deploy
your own buying server down

00:27:40.980 --> 00:27:47.210
in your cloud instance

00:27:47.210 --> 00:27:49.905
OK, we'll look at volumes.

00:27:49.905 --> 00:27:51.280
So right now I
have a test volume

00:27:51.280 --> 00:27:52.405
that was sitting out there.

00:27:54.930 --> 00:27:57.880
Let's go ahead try
to create a volume.

00:27:57.880 --> 00:27:59.680
So you can see here
we have many options,

00:27:59.680 --> 00:28:01.680
but the two that we
really care about today

00:28:01.680 --> 00:28:04.500
are going to be size
and the type of volume

00:28:04.500 --> 00:28:05.380
you want to create.

00:28:05.380 --> 00:28:07.380
So we are going to support
right out of the box.

00:28:07.380 --> 00:28:10.080
We're going to have standard
disks and a flash disk,

00:28:10.080 --> 00:28:11.940
so you'll be able to
create either one when

00:28:11.940 --> 00:28:13.440
creating your data volumes.

00:28:16.510 --> 00:28:18.990
So you can see here we
can type in GN19-Disk,

00:28:18.990 --> 00:28:22.380
we'll give it a size of 20,
and we'll go ahead and use

00:28:22.380 --> 00:28:23.380
a standard disk for now.

00:28:26.322 --> 00:28:27.780
So now our volume
has been created.

00:28:31.358 --> 00:28:33.400
So let's go ahead and
actually create an instance

00:28:33.400 --> 00:28:36.220
on the command line.

00:28:36.220 --> 00:28:37.853
So here's the help for that.

00:28:37.853 --> 00:28:39.270
The main thing to
take away-- it's

00:28:39.270 --> 00:28:41.130
very similar from
earlier on the UI.

00:28:41.130 --> 00:28:45.840
We're going to supply an image,
we're going to supply some

00:28:45.840 --> 00:28:49.440
memory, networks, the
processing you want to use,

00:28:49.440 --> 00:28:51.960
the processor type, which
could be shared or dedicated--

00:28:51.960 --> 00:28:54.540
so today we're going to
actually use shared--

00:28:54.540 --> 00:28:57.120
and then we can also supply how
much memory you want to use.

00:28:57.120 --> 00:28:58.050
And we're going to
go ahead and we're

00:28:58.050 --> 00:29:00.210
going to attach that
volume that we just

00:29:00.210 --> 00:29:04.973
created earlier as a data
volume to this partition.

00:29:04.973 --> 00:29:05.890
So I go and hit enter.

00:29:14.990 --> 00:29:18.470
So you can see here our
instance was created.

00:29:18.470 --> 00:29:20.440
And so I think a message
to take away here

00:29:20.440 --> 00:29:21.970
is when we create
these instances,

00:29:21.970 --> 00:29:23.560
it's being deployed
into a cloud, so

00:29:23.560 --> 00:29:25.190
not everything's instantaneous.

00:29:25.190 --> 00:29:26.690
So right now a lot
of stuff is going

00:29:26.690 --> 00:29:28.970
to be getting provision
in the back end,

00:29:28.970 --> 00:29:31.820
but we can go ahead
and do a list.

00:29:31.820 --> 00:29:34.590
And you can see here, we
now have a Google19-CLI

00:29:34.590 --> 00:29:36.380
that we've just created.

00:29:36.380 --> 00:29:39.220
And let's dive in
a little deeper.

00:29:39.220 --> 00:29:40.095
We can do a describe.

00:29:47.010 --> 00:29:49.410
And you can see
right now of course

00:29:49.410 --> 00:29:53.700
we specified 0.5 processing,
we had four gigs of memory,

00:29:53.700 --> 00:29:55.920
and the big thing important
here now is the statuses

00:29:55.920 --> 00:29:56.795
and the build status.

00:29:56.795 --> 00:29:59.220
So right now, we're still
provisioning this instance.

00:29:59.220 --> 00:30:01.260
So the build status
will change to OK when

00:30:01.260 --> 00:30:03.600
it's ready to start doing
some more operations,

00:30:03.600 --> 00:30:06.300
but some of that information
like the networking--

00:30:06.300 --> 00:30:08.940
that's going to show up
later once it's actually

00:30:08.940 --> 00:30:11.190
been resolved.

00:30:11.190 --> 00:30:13.020
OK.

00:30:13.020 --> 00:30:15.960
But we can go ahead
and pull up a console.

00:30:15.960 --> 00:30:19.408
So just like the UI, you can
run the command for console

00:30:19.408 --> 00:30:21.200
on this instance, and
what it's going to do

00:30:21.200 --> 00:30:24.180
is it's going to return
you a one-time URL.

00:30:24.180 --> 00:30:25.920
And so when you
click on that link,

00:30:25.920 --> 00:30:27.587
you're going to be
brought to the prompt

00:30:27.587 --> 00:30:28.990
here as it's installing AIX.

00:30:28.990 --> 00:30:32.210
So you can see here, from
the CLI, we've gone ahead

00:30:32.210 --> 00:30:34.595
and we've created an
instance, and then

00:30:34.595 --> 00:30:36.470
now we're actually
logging in to the console.

00:30:39.230 --> 00:30:42.930
So we'll give it a few
seconds and then see

00:30:42.930 --> 00:30:44.430
if we can get the
prompt to come up.

00:30:52.030 --> 00:30:54.980
I guess you want me to
to DL par now, yeah, OK.

00:30:54.980 --> 00:30:55.820
OK.

00:30:55.820 --> 00:30:59.270
Actually now I
think I'll switch,

00:30:59.270 --> 00:31:03.150
and let's do some
more-- oh here we go.

00:31:03.150 --> 00:31:04.740
I'll let that run
the background.

00:31:04.740 --> 00:31:09.045
OK, let's go ahead and do
something more interesting

00:31:09.045 --> 00:31:10.670
that's kind of more
available on power.

00:31:13.740 --> 00:31:15.840
So as you you may
know, we can actually

00:31:15.840 --> 00:31:19.400
change resources while the
operating system is running.

00:31:19.400 --> 00:31:21.390
So on power, we're going
to do what we call--

00:31:21.390 --> 00:31:23.990
usually internally we call
it a DL par operation,

00:31:23.990 --> 00:31:27.730
or dynamic partitioning.

00:31:27.730 --> 00:31:30.900
So let's go ahead do a list.

00:31:30.900 --> 00:31:33.940
Let's use the DemoPower one--

00:31:33.940 --> 00:31:35.840
so we'll do a describe
on demo power.

00:31:39.840 --> 00:31:42.870
And you can see here
it has 0.5 processors

00:31:42.870 --> 00:31:45.600
and 4 gigs of memory.

00:31:45.600 --> 00:31:47.585
So let's go ahead and
pull up a console.

00:31:57.510 --> 00:31:58.520
We're going to log in.

00:32:02.030 --> 00:32:04.830
I'll do an LPAR stat
here to show you

00:32:04.830 --> 00:32:06.983
that we have four
gigs of memory.

00:32:06.983 --> 00:32:08.650
Sorry it's hard to
see the prompt here--

00:32:08.650 --> 00:32:12.260
4 gigs of memory
and 0.5 processors.

00:32:12.260 --> 00:32:15.300
I'm going to go ahead
and do a VM stat one--

00:32:15.300 --> 00:32:17.640
so this is just going
to be a simulate just

00:32:17.640 --> 00:32:19.510
like a job running
on this system,

00:32:19.510 --> 00:32:22.530
so we know that it
was actually live.

00:32:22.530 --> 00:32:26.820
Let's go back to my prompt
here, and let's go ahead

00:32:26.820 --> 00:32:29.440
and perform an update
while it's running.

00:32:29.440 --> 00:32:32.052
So let's look at the update
command for instance.

00:32:32.052 --> 00:32:33.760
Right here I think
were really important,

00:32:33.760 --> 00:32:35.802
we want to just change
the processors and memory.

00:32:35.802 --> 00:32:39.540
You can also change the
name, but that really

00:32:39.540 --> 00:32:40.530
wouldn't be a DL par.

00:32:40.530 --> 00:32:44.670
So let's go ahead and
do processors to 0.75,

00:32:44.670 --> 00:32:46.700
and we'll give it six
gigs of memory instead.

00:32:46.700 --> 00:32:49.800
So we're going to go up 0.25
on the processor and two gigs

00:32:49.800 --> 00:32:50.812
on the memory.

00:32:50.812 --> 00:32:52.020
Let's go ahead and hit enter.

00:33:01.210 --> 00:33:02.280
So that's been updated.

00:33:02.280 --> 00:33:05.060
And now we can go back
to our prompt here,

00:33:05.060 --> 00:33:07.480
and while this is
being initiated,

00:33:07.480 --> 00:33:10.120
you're probably going to see a
little message from AIX telling

00:33:10.120 --> 00:33:13.300
you that the processor memory
has been changed-- right there.

00:33:13.300 --> 00:33:17.470
It showed up really
quick, but it's

00:33:17.470 --> 00:33:20.860
telling you that we
have six gigs of memory

00:33:20.860 --> 00:33:22.330
and 0.75 processors.

00:33:22.330 --> 00:33:24.780
So let me go ahead
and stop this prompt.

00:33:24.780 --> 00:33:26.675
I can do an LPAR stat here.

00:33:26.675 --> 00:33:28.050
And you can see
that while it was

00:33:28.050 --> 00:33:33.480
running we've just increased
to six gigs of memory and 0.75

00:33:33.480 --> 00:33:34.940
processors.

00:33:34.940 --> 00:33:37.040
And we can go back to the--

00:33:37.040 --> 00:33:40.430
sorry I keep looking
at the wrong thing--

00:33:40.430 --> 00:33:45.023
to the command line, and
we can do a describe,

00:33:45.023 --> 00:33:46.440
and you'll see
that it's also been

00:33:46.440 --> 00:33:48.900
updated on the command line.

00:33:48.900 --> 00:33:51.020
So now we're matching
what it shows--

00:33:51.020 --> 00:33:56.185
0.75 and six gigs of memory.

00:33:56.185 --> 00:33:58.060
I think also I can show
you-- so I'm actually

00:33:58.060 --> 00:34:02.470
running this pcloud
inside of GCP,

00:34:02.470 --> 00:34:07.430
so I've created an ubuntu
instance in my GCP project,

00:34:07.430 --> 00:34:09.880
so I could technically go
ahead and I can actually

00:34:09.880 --> 00:34:17.420
ping this network all the
way from GCP down into power.

00:34:17.420 --> 00:34:19.040
So this is the
full link running.

00:34:19.040 --> 00:34:22.300
So I'm actually running
pcloud on some random VM

00:34:22.300 --> 00:34:24.850
that I have in
GCP, but I can also

00:34:24.850 --> 00:34:27.730
talk to the network
down in the power kolo.

00:34:27.730 --> 00:34:31.440
So that's the part they talked
about earlier with the VPC

00:34:31.440 --> 00:34:32.469
pairing.

00:34:32.469 --> 00:34:34.239
So it's pretty interesting.

00:34:34.239 --> 00:34:35.560
All of it works.

00:34:35.560 --> 00:34:38.500
We have a fully
fledged CLI and UI.

00:34:38.500 --> 00:34:42.159
So-- oh, yeah, so
the latency-- you

00:34:42.159 --> 00:34:44.639
can see it's a one
second millisecond,

00:34:44.639 --> 00:34:45.880
so it's very, very fast.

00:34:49.760 --> 00:34:51.350
Yeah, so real quick, let's see--

00:35:01.150 --> 00:35:03.120
Just to go back, I
want to show you--

00:35:03.120 --> 00:35:05.722
we created this UI earlier.

00:35:05.722 --> 00:35:07.180
I don't want you
to call me a liar,

00:35:07.180 --> 00:35:10.270
so I want to show you that our
AIX actually did boot up here.

00:35:10.270 --> 00:35:11.530
So there you go.

